g to find the best  angle for a picture that will capture as  much detail as possible while showing us  the most information about the group as  a whole we would typically use this  method to visualize and explore our data  when using this multivariate analysis  method it's important to play around  with the data using normalization and  filtering while paying attention to  outliers for the best possible  interpretation but PCA does not identify  groups or their boundaries and that's  when we have to turn the clustering  there are many types of clustering and  hierarchical is one of the most popular  approaches because it allows clustering  on different levels let's say we have  our samples organized in some space so  imagine the PCA 2d plot where samples  are positioned in some perspective that  best represent the data variability the  algorithm would find the two closest  objects in this projection and then link  them together this is a cluster then we  would find the next two closest group of  two where any two linked objects are  considered as a single object and their  location is the center of that cluster  the algorithm will run until all the  objects are assigned to a cluster with  all the objects in it to run clustering  on the T by wooden foot platform you'll  need to upload the table of expression  that we generated from analyzing the  cell line data this data can be  downloaded directly from the lesson or  from a demo pipeline in the cell line or  in a seat demo the clustering methods  are located in the data mining section  of the platform hierarchical clustering  is an example of an agglomerative or  bottom-up clustering method starts with  attributing each sample to a separate  cluster it then finds the pair of most  similar samples and unites them into one  cluster  this procedure is repeated until coming  into one cluster that contains all the  samples the pipeline will take the  output perform clustering and then put  out the result in several formats that  we will discuss in a little bit but what  are the parameters the linkage parameter  determines the definition for measuring  inter cluster difference single sets the  definition as the distance between the  two nearest points in two clusters or as  complete defines the distance is that  between the two farthest points the two  ward methods are variations of wards  minimum variance method the distance  parameter specifies the distance metric  to use a measuring distance between data  points  these can be Euclidean Manhattan maximum  or kambera the clusters expected count  parameter sets the cutoff height to the  one of the results in a given number of  clusters do transpose allows the user to  transpose the input matrix to set where  the clustered objects or samples are  represented by rows or columns of the  input table we can use this clustering  to cluster objects or samples or even  features which are genes  what is cluster linkage well there are  several types the average linkage method  finds all possible pairwise distances  for points belonging to two different  clusters and then calculates this  average this method is less affected by  outliers than the others single linkage  uses the minimum difference to determine  clustering remember that this method is  also known as nearest neighbor  clustering the distance between two  groups is defined as the distance  between the two closest neighbors and  more formally it finds the minimum  possible distance between points  belonging to two different clusters this  method often yields clusters which  individuals are added sequentially to a  single group the complete linkage method  uses the maximum  and is also known as the furthest  neighbor or Maxima method this method  defines the distance between two groups  as the distance between their farthest  apart members more formally it finds the  maximum possible distance between points  belonging to two different clusters this  method usually yields clusters t